module.exports = [{"Article Number":19,"Title":"On the Trail of the Wolf","Paragraph":1,"Source File":"C:/Users/emhig/OneDrive/OneStop_Txt Files/On-the-trail-of-the-wolf.txt","Elementary":"Robert Myslajek stops. Between two paw prints on a mountain path, the scientist finds what he is looking for. “Droppings!” he says happily. It is so rare to see a wolf that seeing droppings makes it a good day. But it is getting easier to see a wolf. There are now about 1,500 wolves in Poland. The number has doubled in 15 years. The wolf, the brown bear, the lynx and the wolverine are Europe’s last large predator carnivores. Scientists from Britain, Germany and the Netherlands are coming here to find out how Poland has saved wolves who have a bad reputation even in fairy tales.","Intermediate":"Robert Myslajek stops. Between two paw prints on a muddy mountain track, the scientist finds what he is looking for. “Droppings!” he says happily. It is so rare to see a wolf that seeing feces makes it a good day. But it is getting easier. There are now about 1,500 wolves in Poland. The number has doubled in 15 years. Wolves are – along with the brown bear, the lynx and the wolverine – Europe’s last large predator carnivores. Conservationists from Britain, Germany and the Netherlands are coming here to find out how Poland has saved wolves who are criticized even in fairy tales.","Advanced":"Robert Myslajek stops dead. Between two paw prints on a muddy mountain track, the scientist finds what he is looking for. “Droppings!” he enthuses. Wolf sightings are so rare that the sighting of their feces marks a good day, even for a seasoned tracker. But it is getting easier. There are now an estimated 1,500 wolves in Poland. The number has doubled in 15 years. Wolves are – along with the brown bear, the lynx and the wolverine – Europe’s last large predator carnivores. Conservationists from Britain, Germany and the Netherlands are beating a path here to find out how Poland has saved this protected species, slandered even in fairy tales.","Q:":"How does Myslajek react to what he sees between the two paw prints?","Qa:":"He becomes excited","Qb:":"He says they are about to see a wolf","Qc:":"He calls his British, German and Dutch colleagues","Qd:":"He takes out his camera","Q1:":"Why do scientists from Britain, Germany and the Netherlands come to Poland?","Q1a:":"To learn how wolves were saved in Poland","Q1b:":"To help save Poland’s wolves","Q1c:":"To spot and study wolf droppings","Q1d:":"To study the hunting habits of Polish wolves","Q2:":"Why was it “a good day” for Myslajek?","Q2a:":"Because he found droppings","Q2b:":"Because he spotted a wolf","Q2c:":"Because he learned that the number of wolves in Poland doubled in 15 years","Q2d:":"Because he managed to escape a wolf"},{"Article Number":19,"Title":"On the Trail of the Wolf","Paragraph":2,"Source File":"C:/Users/emhig/OneDrive/OneStop_Txt Files/On-the-trail-of-the-wolf.txt","Elementary":"The scientist says wolves can move up to 30 kilometers during a single hunt. “The pack that he is tracking is a strong group of eight or nine animals. This year, we have recorded five cubs, two young wolves and two adults. We track them using special cameras in the forest and by following their prints in the mud and snow. In each family group, only one pair of adults has cubs each year. All the wolves in the pack look after the young.” Myslajek, the son of a shepherd, doesn’t understand wolves’ bad reputation. “Why did we have to have the fairy tale Little Red Riding Hood, with its big bad wolf?” He is fascinated by these animals, who remained in the wild 33,000 years ago when other types of dog decided on a much more comfortable life as pets.","Intermediate":"The scientist says wolves can move up to 30 kilometers during a single hunt. “The Beskidy pack is a strong unit, eight or nine animals. This year, we have recorded five cubs, two young wolves and two adults. We track them using motion cameras in the forest and by following their prints in the mud and snow. In each family group, only one pair of adults has cubs each year. All pack members care for the young.” Myslajek, the son of a shepherd, doesn’t understand wolves’ bad reputation. “Why did we have to have the fairy tale Little Red Riding Hood, with its big bad wolf?’’ He is fascinated by these aloof animals who remained in the wild 33,000 years ago when others decided on a much more comfortable life as domestic dogs.","Advanced":"The scientist, who is a familiar face on Polish television, says wolves are exceptional animals that are capable of moving up to 30km during a single hunt. “The Beskidy pack is a strong unit, eight or nine individuals. This year, we have recorded five cubs, two yearlings and two adults. We track them using motion-activated cameras in the forest and by following their prints in the mud and snow. In each family group, only one pair reproduces, once a year. All pack members care for the young with solidarity and devotion.” Myslajek, the son of a shepherd, is puzzled by wolves’ bad reputation. “Why does one speak of a 'lone-wolf gunman'? Why did we have to have Little Red Riding Hood, with its big bad wolf?” He is fascinated by these aloof canines who remained in the wild 33,000 years ago when others decided on a much more comfortable existence as domestic dogs.","Q:":"What is one method scientists use to keep track of the wolves?","Qa:":"They follow the wolves’ tracks","Qb:":"They place cameras on the wolves","Qc:":"They use dogs","Qd:":"They use cameras mounted on drones","Q1:":"What does Myslajek think of fairy tales such as Little Red Riding Hood?","Q1a:":"He disagrees with the image of wolves depicted in them","Q1b:":"He thinks people misunderstand them","Q1c:":"He thinks they accurately depict how pack wolves care for the young","Q1d:":"He thinks they are a fun way to teach children about wolves","Q2:":"How are mud and snow useful for the scientists?","Q2a:":"They allow the scientists to see wolf tracks better","Q2b:":"They make wolves more visible on the cameras in the forest","Q2c:":"They make it easier to distinguish wolf tracks from dog tracks","Q2d:":"They help preserve wolf droppings for study"},{"Article Number":19,"Title":"On the Trail of the Wolf","Paragraph":3,"Source File":"C:/Users/emhig/OneDrive/OneStop_Txt Files/On-the-trail-of-the-wolf.txt","Elementary":"Myslajek says only the need to save forests and control the wild animal population can save Europe’s wild carnivores, especially the unpopular wolf. “Natural predators balance the ecosystem. They reduce the number of herbivores, which allows trees to grow tall for birds to nest in.” The ban on wolf hunting in the western Carpathian mountains became law in 1995 and in the whole of Poland in 1998. There are now wolf packs in nearly all the country’s major forests where the wolves exist together with humans.","Intermediate":"Myslajek says only scientific arguments – the need to regenerate forests and control the wild animal population – can save Europe’s wild carnivores, especially the unpopular wolf. “Natural predators balance the ecosystem. They reduce the number of herbivores, which allows trees to grow tall for birds to nest in.” The ban on wolf hunting in the western Carpathian mountains became law in 1995 and in the whole of Poland in 1998. There are now wolf packs in nearly all the country’s major forests where the wolves coexist with humans.","Advanced":"Wolves are not pooches. Myslajek says only scientific arguments – the need to regenerate forests and control the wild animal population – can save Europe’s wild carnivores, especially the unpopular wolf. “Natural predators balance the ecosystem. They keep herbivores in check, thus allowing trees to grow tall for birds to nest in.” The ban on wolf hunting in the western Carpathian mountains came into force in 1995 and nationwide in Poland in 1998. There are now resident packs in virtually all the country’s major forests. The predators coexist with humans rather than being fenced off, as they are in African safari parks.","Q:":"What is a result of the predatory habits of wolves?","Qa:":"It allows birds to have tall trees to nest in","Qb:":"It reduces the number of birds in the ecosystem","Qc:":"It allows wolf packs to form in major forests","Qd:":"It prevents harmful invasive species from thriving","Q1:":"What is a consequence of the wolf-hunting ban?","Q1a:":"Humans and wolves exist peacefully together in forests","Q1b:":"Wolf packs avoid humans across the country’s major forests","Q1c:":"More herbivores can thrive in the ecosystem","Q1d:":"More humans are attacked by wolves","Q2:":"Where was wolf-hunting banned in 1995?","Q2a:":"The western Carpathian mountains","Q2b:":"The whole country of Poland","Q2c:":"Most of Poland’s major forests","Q2d:":"Forests with no human population"},{"Article Number":19,"Title":"On the Trail of the Wolf","Paragraph":4,"Source File":"C:/Users/emhig/OneDrive/OneStop_Txt Files/On-the-trail-of-the-wolf.txt","Elementary":"The Polish government pays compensation to farmers when a wolf kills their farm animals. Myslajek tells farmers to put up electric fences. He also tells them to use two things that wolves find scary: strings of small red flags (to keep wolves away from sheep) and the Tatra Mountain Sheepdog.","Intermediate":"The Polish government pays compensation for farm animals killed by wolves. Myslajek advises farmers to put up electric fences. He has helped bring back the use of two deterrents that, for reasons no one understands, wolves find very scary: strings of small red flags (that you hang around sheep pens) and the Tatra Mountain Sheepdog.","Advanced":"The Polish government pays compensation for livestock killed by wolves. Myslajek advises farmers on erecting electric fences. He has helped revive the use of two deterrents that, for reasons no one quite understands, wolves find particularly scary: strings of small red flags (hung around sheep pens) and the bark of the fluffy white Tatra Mountain Sheepdog.","Q:":"What does the Polish government do when a wolf kills a farm animal?","Qa:":"Provides money to the owner of the animal","Qb:":"Compensates the farmer with resources for killing wolves","Qc:":"Provides Tatra Mountain Sheepdogs to guard the farm","Qd:":"Reduces the farm-owner’s taxes","Q1:":"What is one of the things that can help in scaring off wolves, according to Myslajek?","Q1a:":"Owning a Tatra Mountain Sheepdog","Q1b:":"Hanging small red strings","Q1c:":"Constructing larger fences","Q1d:":"Setting up traps around their farms","Q2:":"What is common to red flags and the Tatra Mountain Sheepdog?","Q2a:":"Both are scary to wolves","Q2b:":"Both are used by Myslajek","Q2c:":"Both are recommended to farm owners by the Polish government","Q2d:":"Both have been used for centuries in Poland"},{"Article Number":19,"Title":"On the Trail of the Wolf","Paragraph":5,"Source File":"C:/Users/emhig/OneDrive/OneStop_Txt Files/On-the-trail-of-the-wolf.txt","Elementary":"Poland didn’t have many big roads until recently and this has helped wolves. In 1989, when the communists left power, Poland had only one motorway. Major road projects began after Poland joined the European Union in 2004 and they now have to consider wild animals. The way people think about wolves has also changed. “For many years, hunting was a part of the culture in Poland. In 1975, there were fewer than 100 wolves in Poland.” Myslajek says that Polish wolves are much safer now but they are not completely safe. Packs go into neighboring countries – Russia, Lithuania and Belarus – where there is still hunting.","Intermediate":"Poland’s wolves have been helped by the country’s late infrastructure development. In 1989, when the communists left power, Poland had only one motorway. Major road projects began after Poland joined the European Union in 2004 and they are required to consider wild animals. Attitudes have also changed. “For many years, hunting was cultural. In 1975, there were fewer than 100 wolves in Poland. From the 1950s, hunting wolves was encouraged by the authorities. They paid a reward for killing a wolf worth a month’s salary. It was carnage.’’ Myslajek says that Polish wolves are much safer now but they are not completely safe. Packs of wolves cross country borders and hunting still happens in neighboring Russia, Lithuania and Belarus.","Advanced":"The survival of Poland’s wolves has been helped by the country’s belated infrastructure development. In 1989, when the communists relinquished power, Poland had only one motorway. Major road projects – requiring wildlife impact studies – began after Poland joined the European Union in 2004. The country now has one of the highest densities in the world of overhead crossings and underpasses for wild animals. Attitudes have also changed. “For many years, hunting was cultural. In 1975, there were fewer than 100 wolves in Poland. Beginning in the 1950s, hunting wolves had been encouraged by the authorities. They paid a reward for killing a wolf worth a month’s salary. It was carnage.” Myslajek says the improvement in Polish wolves’ survival chances has been considerable but remains fragile. Packs are mobile across borders and hunting still goes on in neighboring Russia, Lithuania and Belarus.","Q:":"What was true about Poland in 1975?","Qa:":"The wolf population was smaller than 100","Qb:":"The way people thought about wolves started changing","Qc:":"The communists were no longer in power","Qd:":"The Polish government introduced new laws to protect wolves","Q1:":"Why does Myslajek mention Russia, Lithuania and Belarus?","Q1a:":"Because Polish wolves cross the border to these countries","Q1b:":"Because wolves are safer there than in Poland","Q1c:":"Because they have fewer roads than Poland has","Q1d:":"Because they are learning from Poland how to protect wolves","Q2:":"Why were there fewer than 100 wolves in Poland in 1975?","Q2a:":"Hunting was a widespread practice in Poland around that time","Q2b:":"The way people thought about wolves in Poland changed around that time","Q2c:":"Most Polish wolves had migrated to Russia, Lithuania and Belarus","Q2d:":"There was a drought making it hard for them to survive"},{"Article Number":19,"Title":"On the Trail of the Wolf","Paragraph":6,"Source File":"C:/Users/emhig/OneDrive/OneStop_Txt Files/On-the-trail-of-the-wolf.txt","Elementary":"He says Poland’s new government doesn’t like wolves. “The Environment Minister, Jan Szyszko, is a hunter. There are 120,000 hunters in Poland and they have a lot of influence. It’s not easy to defend wolves. You can’t say to the politicians that wolves are a big tourist attraction. Most tourists want to see the animals but wolves stay away from humans. They have a very strong sense of smell.”","Intermediate":"He says Poland’s new government, elected in October 2015, doesn’t like wolves. “The Environment Minister, Jan Szyszko, is a hunter. There are 120,000 licensed hunters in Poland and they have a lot of influence. Being a wolf advocate is not easy. You can’t argue to the politicians that wolves are a big tourist attraction. Most tourists want to see the animals but wolves stay away from humans. They have a very sensitive sense of smell.’’","Advanced":"He claims Poland’s new government, elected in October 2015, is hostile to wolves. “The Environment Minister, Jan Szyszko, makes no secret of being a hunter. There are 120,000 licensed hunters in Poland and they are influential in parliament. Being a wolf advocate is not easy. It is not as if you can argue to the politicians that wolves are a big tourist attraction. Most tourists want to see the animals but wolves stay away from humans. They have a tremendously sensitive sense of smell.”","Q:":"Why is it hard to support wolves in Poland’s new government?","Qa:":"Wolf hunters have a large influence in the government","Qb:":"Only the Environment Minister opposes wolf hunting","Qc:":"Politicians won’t acknowledge that wolves attract a large number of tourists","Qd:":"The new government is prioritizing other issues","Q1:":"Why are wolves not a big tourist attraction?","Q1a:":"Wolves don’t want to be near humans","Q1b:":"Humans want to stay away from wolves","Q1c:":"Hunters are opposed to wolf tourism","Q1d:":"Other wild animals are more interesting to tourists","Q2:":"Who is Jan Szyszko?","Q2a:":"A minister in the new Polish government","Q2b:":"An activist who opposes hunting","Q2c:":"A tourist in Poland","Q2d:":"A Polish farm owner"}]